Shortcut Trick

In a **parallelogram**, **opposite angles** are equal and **adjacent angles** are supplementary (sum is 180&deg;).

Given angle = 70&deg;

Opposite angle = 70&deg;

Adjacent angle = 180&deg; &minus; 70&deg; = 110&deg;

Angle opposite to the adjacent angle = 110&deg;

The other three angles are 70&deg;, 110&deg;, and 110&deg;.

**&there4; The correct answer is 70&deg;, 110&deg;, 110&deg;.**

**Alternate Method**

**Given:**

One angle of the **parallelogram** = 70&deg;

**Formula used:**

1. Sum of **adjacent angles** of a parallelogram = 180&deg;

2. **Opposite angles** of a parallelogram are equal.

Let the **parallelogram** be **ABCD** where &ang;A = 70&deg;.

&rArr; &ang;A + &ang;B = 180&deg; (Adjacent angles are supplementary)

&rArr; 70&deg; + &ang;B = 180&deg;

&rArr; &ang;B = 180&deg; &minus; 70&deg;

&rArr; &ang;B = 110&deg;

Since **opposite angles** are equal:

&rArr; &ang;C = &ang;A = 70&deg;

&rArr; &ang;D = &ang;B = 110&deg;

&there4; The other three angles are 110&deg;, 70&deg;, and 110&deg; (which can be written as 70&deg;, 110&deg;, 110&deg;).

**&there4; The correct answer is 70&deg;, 110&deg;, 110&deg;.**

**Additional Information**

Sum of Interior Angles

For any quadrilateral, including a **parallelogram**, the sum of all **interior angles** is always 360&deg;.

Properties of Diagonals

The **diagonals** of a **parallelogram** bisect each other, although they are not necessarily equal in length unless it is a rectangle.

Consecutive Angles

Any pair of **consecutive angles** in a **parallelogram** will always sum to 180&deg; because they are interior angles on the same side of a transversal cutting parallel lines.

